T current expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":131931,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":4961000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":36595,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":40086,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2437.78,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":135.74,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":{"summary":"Broadland's Annual Governance Statement review for 2025/26, produced in a revised format under the CIPFA/SOLACE Framework Addendum that now requires an explicit conclusion on whether governance is 'fit for purpose'. The review concludes the council's arrangements are fit for purpose with no significant governance issues \u2014 only minor control weaknesses, which are listed in section 6 of the AGS itself (an appendix not included in this paper). The 2025/26 internal audit opinion on governance, risk management and control was still being finalised and was to be confirmed at the 30 July meeting. The paper comes from the officer team Broadland shares with South Norfolk but is addressed to Broadland's own Audit Committee; nothing in it signals financial stress.","fy":"2025/26","as_of_doc":"Annual Governance Statement 2025/26","as_of_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16389/260730 REP Annual Governance Statement.pdf","as_of_date":"2026-07-30","signals":[{"code":"none","headline":"2025/26 governance review concludes arrangements are fit for purpose with no significant governance issues and only minor control weaknesses; internal audit opinion to be confirmed at the 30 July 2026 committee","why":"An explicitly clean fit-for-purpose conclusion under the tougher new AGS format leaves nothing here for a lender to chase."}],"key_figures":[],"commercial":null,"tms_components":null},"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[{"title":"Annual Governance Statement 2025/26","url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16389/260730 REP Annual Governance Statement.pdf","committee":"Broadland Audit Committee","date":"2026-07-30","category":"accounts","fy":"2025/26"}],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Broadland's welfare support costs rise 11 per cent but council warns of cutting risks","lead":"Broadland pays a share of Council Tax Reduction - support to help low-income households pay their council tax bills. Its share rose 11 per cent over two years, from \u00a3541,100 in 2024-25 to \u00a3601,300 in 2026-27, according to a report to the South Norfolk Communities Committee.\n\nThe increase is not from more people needing support: caseloads remain stable, and almost all working-age claimants receive Universal Credit, the main welfare benefit for working-age people. The growth comes entirely from rising council tax levels year-on-year.\n\nBroadland's analysis shows why cutting this support would be counterproductive. The council states any reduction \"may not generate an equivalent increase in collectable Council Tax income and may instead increase hardship, debt and recovery costs\". The council recognises that welfare support and tax collection are linked: cutting one damages the other.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Council Tax Reduction Scheme 202728","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16877/16877.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"South Norfolk Communities Committee","paper_title":"Council Tax Reduction Scheme 202728","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16877/16877.pdf","headline":"Council identifies that reducing Council Tax Reduction support could increase collection losses and enforcement costs, offsetting anticipated savings.","detail":"The paper notes that 98-99 per cent of working-age CTR recipients receive Universal Credit (extremely vulnerable population). Section 6.5 explicitly states that reducing CTR support 'may not generate an equivalent increase in collectable Council Tax income and may instead increase hardship, debt and recovery costs'. This reveals a direct economic link between welfare support and tax collection effectiveness.","category":"income","matters":"A lender would need to understand that council tax collection depends on welfare support infrastructure; reductions could worsen net financial position through arrears and enforcement costs.","figures":"98-99 per cent of working-age claimants on Universal Credit","quote":"Any reduction or removal of working-age CTR support would need to be considered carefully to ensure there is not an adverse impact on Council Tax collection.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"South Norfolk Communities Committee","paper_title":"Council Tax Reduction Scheme 202728","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16877/16877.pdf","headline":"SNC's share of Council Tax Reduction costs has grown 11 per cent over two years, from \u00a3541,100 to \u00a3601,300, as council tax levels rise.","detail":"The council's share of total CTR costs grew from \u00a3541,100 in 2024/25 to \u00a3577,500 in 2025/26 to \u00a3601,300 in 2026/27. The paper notes caseloads remain stable; the growth is entirely driven by council tax rises year-on-year. The pattern is expected to continue as council tax increases.","category":"demand","matters":"A lender would want to understand the council's growing welfare commitments and how they scale with council tax increases, affecting budget sustainability.","figures":"\u00a3541,100 (2024/25), \u00a3577,500 (2025/26), \u00a3601,300 (2026/27); approximately 11 per cent growth over two years","quote":"The increase in spend is primarily due to rising Council Tax charges rather than growth in caseload, as caseloads remain at similar levels.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-30","committee":"Broadland Audit Committee","paper_title":"260730 REP Annual Governance Statement APP A","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16390/16390.pdf","headline":"Broadland Growth Limited now wholly council-owned with no active development sites following NORSE withdrawal","detail":"NORSE withdrew from joint ownership of Broadland Growth Limited on 31 March 2025, making the company wholly owned by the council. The company has completed two developments historically but currently operates no live sites. Unclear what financial exposure or asset write-down this represents.","category":"capital","matters":"Changes in company ownership structure and project activity can indicate financial exposure or asset impairment risks that affect council balance sheet","figures":"31 March 2025 NORSE withdrawal; two developments delivered to date; currently no live sites","quote":"Broadland Growth Limited was a jointly owned company with NORSE. However, on 31 March 2025 Norse withdrew so that Broadland Growth Limited is now a wholly owned company. The company has delivered two developments to date. However currently, the company has no live sites.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-30","committee":"Broadland Audit Committee","paper_title":"260730 REP Annual Governance Statement APP A","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16390/16390.pdf","headline":"No formal governance review yet undertaken for council-owned and associated companies","detail":"The council has not conducted a formal review of client-side governance arrangements for Broadland Living Ltd and its involvement in Norfolk Environmental Credits Ltd joint venture. An internal audit review is scheduled for 2026/27 to assess whether roles, responsibilities and reporting mechanisms remain fit for purpose.","category":"governance","matters":"Lack of periodic formal review of company governance creates risk that oversight and accountability mechanisms may not adequately protect council interests or manage company exposures","figures":"Review planned 2026/27","quote":"A formal review of the Council's client-side governance arrangements for its companies has not yet been undertaken. Given the importance of effective oversight, transparency and risk management in relation to Council-owned or associated companies, it is important that these arrangements are periodically reviewed to ensure roles, responsibilities and reporting mechanisms remain clear, robust and fit for purpose.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-30","committee":"Broadland Audit Committee","paper_title":"260730 REP Annual Governance Statement APP A","paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16390/16390.pdf","headline":"Internal audit recommendations not consistently implemented within agreed timelines","detail":"Managers reported that audit recommendations were not always implemented on time. While Section 151 Officer and Monitoring Officer oversight produced in-year improvements, the issue remains flagged for continued monitoring through 2026/27. This indicates control weaknesses are not being resolved promptly.","category":"audit","matters":"Delayed fixes to identified control weaknesses mean governance gaps persist longer than planned, delaying mitigation of identified risks","figures":"Ongoing monitoring through 2026/27","quote":"Some managers highlighted this is an area whereby recommendations were not always implemented in line with the agreed timeline. This is something that the Section 151 Officer and Monitoring Officer have kept a close eye on and in year improvements have been realised. This will continue to be monitored.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Broadland","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Council identifies that reducing Council Tax Reduction support could increase collection losses and enforcement costs, offsetting anticipated savings.","lead":"A lender would need to understand that council tax collection depends on welfare support infrastructure; reductions could worsen net financial position through arrears and enforcement costs.","also":["1 other finding in this window"],"watch":null,"n_findings":2,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Council Tax Reduction Scheme 202728","top_paper_url":"https://southnorfolkandbroadland.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16877/16877.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":59,"news_drafts_total":1}}